<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Fa23-Midterm2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math 234 - Midterm 2 - Fall 2023</title>
	<exercise points="12">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface <m>z=e^{x y}+x^3 y</m> at the point <m>(0,3,1).</m>
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				The equation of the tangent plane to <m>z=f(x, y)</m> at <m>(0,3,1)</m> is given by
				<me>z-1=f_x(0,3) \cdot x+f_y(0,3)(y-3).</me>
				Here,
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					f_x \amp = y e^{x y}+3 x^2 y \\
					f_y \amp = x e^{x y}+x^3 \\
					f_x(0,3) \amp = 3 \\
					f_y(0,3) \amp = 0
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				The equation of the tangent plane is
				<me>z-1=3 x.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <me>f(x, y)=x^2+4 y^2-2 x y+4</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="7">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find all critical point(s) of <m>f(x, y).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{cases}
						f_x=2 x-2 y=0 \\
						f_y=8 y-2 x=0
						\end{cases}
					</me>
From the first equation, <m>y=x.</m> Substituting into the second equation gives <m>8 x-2 x=0 \implies x=0.</m> The only critical point is <m>(0,0).</m>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="7">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Classify each critical point you found in (a) as a local maximum, local minimum, or saddle point.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f_{x x} \amp = 2 \\
						f_{y y} \amp = 8 \\
						f_{x y} \amp = -2.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					At <m>(0,0),</m>
<me>D=f_{x x} f_{y y}-f_{x y}^2=2 \cdot 8-(-2)^2=16-4=12 \gt 0.</me>
					Since <m>f_{x x}(0,0)=2 \gt 0,</m> there is a local min at <m>(0,0).</m>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <m>f(x, y)=\sin \left(x^2-y\right).</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute <m>\nabla f(2,4).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f_x \amp = \cos \left(x^2-y\right) \cdot 2 x \\
						f_y \amp = \cos \left(x^2-y\right)(-1) \\
						f_x(2,4) \amp = \cos (0) \cdot 4=4 \\
						f_y(2,4) \amp = \cos (0)(-1)=-1.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					Then, <me>\nabla f(2,4)=\langle 4,-1\rangle.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the directional derivative of <m>f</m> at the point <m>(2,4)</m> in the direction of the vector <m>\vec{v}=\langle 1,-2\rangle.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{v} \amp = \langle 1,-2\rangle \\
						|\vec{v}| \amp = \sqrt{1+4}=\sqrt{5} \\
						D_{\vec{u}} f(2,4) \amp = \langle 4, -1 \rangle \cdot \langle 1 / \sqrt{5}, -2 / \sqrt{5} \rangle = \frac{4}{\sqrt{5}} + \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} = \frac{6}{\sqrt{5}}.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this problem, we want to find the (absolute) maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x, y, z)=x+2 y+4 z</m> subject to the constraint <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=21.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Set up the system of equations that you need to solve to find the maximum and minimum values using the method of Lagrange multipliers, using <m>\lambda</m> as the parameter.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need <m>\nabla f=\lambda \nabla g,</m> and <m>g(x, y, z)=21,</m> where <m>g(x, y, z)=x^2+y^2+z^2.</m> That is, we need to solve
					<me>
						\begin{cases}
						1=2 \lambda x \\
						2=2 \lambda y \\
						4=2 \lambda z \\
						x^2+y^2+z^2=21
						\end{cases}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					There are two values of <m>\lambda</m> (let's call them <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m>) that satisfy your system of equations. Find them.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
From the first Lagrange equation, we know <m>\lambda \neq 0.</m> We can write <m>x=\frac{1}{2 \lambda}, y=\frac{1}{\lambda}, z=\frac{2}{\lambda}.</m> Plugging into the constraint, we get
					<me>\frac{1}{4 \lambda^2}+\frac{1}{\lambda^2}+\frac{4}{\lambda^2}=21.</me>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\frac{1+4+16}{4 \lambda^2} \amp = 21 \\
						\frac{21}{4 \lambda^2} \amp = 21 \implies \lambda^2 = \frac{1}{4}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					<me>\lambda_1=1 / 2, \quad \lambda_2=-1 / 2.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					The two values <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m> you found in part (b) give corresponding points (<m>x_1, y_1, z_1</m>) and (<m>x_2, y_2, z_2</m>). Find them.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					When <m>\lambda=1 / 2,</m> we have <m>x=\frac{1}{2 \lambda}=1, y=\frac{1}{\lambda}=2, z=\frac{2}{\lambda}=4.</m>
				</p>
				<p>
					When <m>\lambda=-1 / 2,</m> we have <m>x=\frac{1}{2 \lambda}=-1, y=\frac{1}{\lambda}=-2, z=\frac{2}{\lambda}=-4.</m>
					<me>\left(x_1, y_1, z_1\right)=(1,2,4), \quad \left(x_2, y_2, z_2\right)=(-1,-2,-4).</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="2">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Identify the absolute maximum and minimum values.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f(1,2,4) \amp = 1+4+16=21 \\
						f(-1,-2,-4) \amp = -1-4-16=-21
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					The absolute max is 21 and the absolute min is -21.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Compute the following integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_0^1 \int_0^y 2 x y^2 d x d y</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int_0^1 \int_0^y 2 x y^2 \, d x \, d y \amp = \int_0^1 \left(\left.x^2 y^2\right|_0^y\right) d y \\
						\amp = \int_0^1\left(y^2 y^2-0\right) d y = \int_0^1 y^4 d y \\
						\amp = \left.\frac{y^5}{5}\right|_0^1 = \frac{1}{5}.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_0^1 \int_{3 y}^3 e^{x^2} d x d y</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need to change the order of integration:
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						3 y \amp \le x \le 3 \\
						0 \amp \le y \le 1
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int_0^1 \int_{3 y}^3 e^{x^2} d x d y \amp = \int_0^3 \int_0^{x / 3} e^{x^2} d y d x \\
						\amp = \int_0^3\left(\left.y e^{x^2}\right|_{y=0}^{y=x / 3}\right) d x = \int_0^3 \frac{1}{3} x e^{x^2} d x \\
						\amp = \int_0^9 \frac{1}{3} \cdot \frac{1}{2} e^u d u \\
						\amp = \left.\frac{1}{6} e^u\right|_0^9=\frac{1}{6} e^9-\frac{1}{6}.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Sketch the region <m>\mathcal{R}</m> and then write down the double integral of <m>f(x, y)</m> over the region <m>\mathcal{R}.</m> You do NOT need to evaluate the integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					The region <m>\mathcal{R}</m> is the region in the first-quadrant bounded by <m>y=x,</m> the <m>y</m>-axis, <m>y=\sqrt{4-x^2},</m> and <m>y=\sqrt{25-x^2}</m> and <m>f(x, y)=\left(x^2+y^2\right)^8.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Use polar coordinates. <m>f(r \cos \theta, r \sin \theta)=\left(r^2\right)^8=r^{16}.</m> Then,
					<me>\iint_{\mathcal{R}} f(x, y) d x d y = \int_{\pi / 4}^{\pi / 2} \int_2^5 r^{16} \cdot r d r d \theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					The region <m>\mathcal{R}</m> is the region in the <m>xy</m>-plane bounded by the three curves <m>x=2, y=x^3-1,</m> and <m>y=\frac{3}{4} x-\frac{10}{8}</m> and <m>f(x, y)=x^2-y^2.</m> (Hint: Observe that <m>y=\frac{3}{4} x-\frac{10}{8}</m> is the tangent line to <m>y=x^3-1</m> at the point <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{-7}{8}\right)</m>)
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>\iint_{\mathcal{R}} f(x, y) d x d y = \int_{1 / 2}^2 \int_{3x/4 - 10/8}^{x^3-1} (x^2-y^2) d y d x.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Write down triple integrals that represent the volume of the following solids. You do NOT need to evaluate the integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					The solid that lies above the cone <m>z=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and below the hemisphere <m>z=\sqrt{1-\left(x^2+y^2\right)}</m> (the non-negative square root).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Using spherical coordinates, we have
					<me>V=\int_0^{2 \pi} \int_0^{\pi / 4} \int_0^1 \rho^2 \sin \phi d \rho d \phi d \theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					The solid that lies between the cylinders <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> and <m>x^2+y^2=4,</m> above the plane <m>z=0,</m> and below the plane <m>3 x+2 y+z=12.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Using Cylindrical coordinates, we can write
					<me>V=\int_0^{2 \pi} \int_1^2 \int_0^{12-3 r \cos \theta-2 r \sin \theta} r d z d r d \theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
